{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 22, :comments ["<b>Thus says HaShem: My son, My firstborn.</b> One needs to understand the intent of this prophecy, which was not said at its [proper] time, and also what safeguard He provided lest Moses err and say this prophecy to Pharaoh the first time, when he stood before Pharaoh." "<b>And it appears</b> that the supernal El devised wisely to remove a stumbling block from Moses' heart, so that he would not recoil from the mission and — heaven forbid — an evil trait would be born in his mind upon seeing the intensity of the plagues while Pharaoh did not budge or stir, with one month entering and another going out. Therefore He placed a statement in his mouth to serve as a guarantee, so that he would not think wicked thoughts — heaven forbid — and He said to him: <b>And you shall say to Pharaoh: Thus says HaShem, My son, My firstborn</b>. From now on Moses would look forward to this thing, and as long as he had not yet seen the plague of the firstborn he would steady his heart and not be afraid, as long as he had not yet brought that plague upon him. And the scope of the prophecy is as follows: ״When you go to return to Egypt, see…״ and so on, and ״I am informing you that I will harden his heart and he will not send them forth even after several plagues, and ultimately ״you shall say to him… Here I am slaying״ and so on.״" "<b>Further,</b> He devised wisely in this to inform Moses that this plague would be the last one, for through it Pharaoh would send the people forth, since HaShem did not say that He would harden Pharaoh's heart after the plague of the firstborn. And since this is so, when Pharaoh said to Moses (Exodus 10:28): ״You shall not see my face again,״ he recognized that the time of the command that HaShem had told him on the way when he came from Midian to Egypt had arrived: ״And you shall say: My son, My firstborn.״ And although he added things that were not stated here, presumably they were stated, and what is unstated is inferred from what is explicit. And with this there is no room to ask: when was that prophecy said to Moses, that our Sages needed to offer midrashic explanations — for here it is before you. And HaShem was not concerned that Moses might err and say this prophecy to Pharaoh, because at the moment Moses would report Pharaoh's words before HaShem, immediately and without delay He would send him on a mission for another plague, and consequently Moses would know that the time of the plague of the firstborn had not yet arrived until the moment came when Pharaoh spoke and said to him: ״You shall not see my face again.״ And perceiving that he could not go out from before Pharaoh to prophesy outside the city to know what HaShem would speak, he said: the time has arrived to declare the plague of the firstborn. Or, one may infer precisely from the words of HaShem, who said: ״And I will harden Pharaoh's heart״ — and from now on one does not know the measure to which HaShem would harden his heart, that He would say to him afterward [regarding] the plague of the firstborn. And when he said to him: ״You shall not see my face again,״ he recognized that this is the endpoint of the hardening of the heart, and in combination with the first reason that we have written, he declared to him the mission of the plague of the firstborn." "<b>Further,</b> it is possible that if Pharaoh had maintained his resolve not to send Israel forth and had not spoken words against the honor of the Blessed One, as he said (Exodus 5:2): ״Who is HaShem,״ HaShem would have brought the plague of the firstborn upon him immediately, without delay, and would not have made him drink the cups of poison. And this is what HaShem alluded to Moses when He said: ״And I will harden his heart and he will not send the people forth״ — meaning that the hardening is for the purpose of not sending, for if he would send forth, even the plague of the firstborn would not come upon him. And when the matter would be nothing but the withholding of the people's release, the plague of the firstborn would suffice as a punishment. But after he offended His honor — may He be blessed — great and verifiable plagues would come upon him. And so you will find that HaShem said (Exodus 9:16): ״In order to show you My power and that My name may be declared״ and so on."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:24:44.925559751Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}